   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to notification by an\nexcavator of damage to an underground facility",
    "basePrintNo": "S5163",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5163-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §764, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5163\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the general business law, in relation\nto notification by an excavator of damage to an underground facility\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo require excavators to call 911 in the event of an emergency.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends subdivision 5 of section 764 of the general business\nlaw, as amended by Chapter 333 of the laws of 2018, to require excava-\ntors to immediately notify 911 in the event of an electrical short or\nthe escape of gas or hazardous fluids endangering life.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation amends chapter 333 of the laws of 2018 to require exca-\nvators to immediately notify 911, instead of the fire department, in the\nevent of an electrical short or escape of gas or hazardous fluids endan-\ngering life. Calling 911 is preferable during such an emergency and will\nensure the most prompt response, since fire departments throughout the\nstate do not always have someone at all hours answering phone calls.\nRequiring excavators to notify the fire department instead of 911 would\ncause a delay in getting first responders to a scene. Even if a fire\ndepartment does answer a call, the call would still need to be\ndispatched over to 911, taking critical time away during an emergency.\n\nIn addition, this bill will make the law consistent with current state\nregulations, which require excavators to immediately notify 911 in the\nevent of an emergency. (See 16 NYCRR 753-3.14). Every moment counts\nduring an emergency and requiring that calls go to 911 w ill better\nensure the safety of those working in the field as well as the impacted\npublic.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as\nsection 6 of Chapter 333 of the laws of 2018, takes effect.\n",
